\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23.2}{\ignorespaces Sequence-of-returns risk. Two retirees start with \$1 million and withdraw \$40,000 a year in real terms. Both experience the same thirty annual returns, three years of $-15\%$ and twenty-seven of $+7\%$, in opposite orders. With the bad years first, the portfolio falls to \$85,000; with the bad years last, it ends at \$1.77 million.}}{277}{figure.caption.78}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:24-sequence}{{23.2}{277}{Sequence-of-returns risk. Two retirees start with \$1 million and withdraw \$40,000 a year in real terms. Both experience the same thirty annual returns, three years of $-15\%$ and twenty-seven of $+7\%$, in opposite orders. With the bad years first, the portfolio falls to \$85,000; with the bad years last, it ends at \$1.77 million}{figure.caption.78}{}}
